Founder Tom Joule said the investment would help Joules open 16 new stores in the coming year
Casual clothing label Joules is aiming to boost its expansion plans with a £22m investment from private equity firm Lloyds Development Capital (LDC).
Tom Joule, who founded the company in 1989 selling socks, wellies and knitwear at outdoor events, said the investment would help Joules open 16 new stores in the coming year, expand into new product categories including toiletries and step up e-commerce activity overseas. The retailer, which is based in Market Harborough, Leicestershire, already operates two stores in South Korea via a franchise partner and also sells online and wholesale in Germany, France and the US.
"We have got a robust five-year plan and this gives us the confidence and security to deliver that," Joule said.
The brand, which has 76 shops and an online store as well as selling wholesale to hundreds of independent shops and department stores including Selfridges and John Lewis, more than doubled pre-tax profits to £3.7m in the year to May while sales rose 19% to £79.4m. That trend continued with a 23% jump in sales in the first five months of the new financial year and the company is expected to achieve sales of around £90m.
