· Drive to cut emissions in British skies by 10% · Airports and airlines 'will have to work together'
The way aircraft take off, fly and land in Britain will undergo a significant overhaul as part of plans to cut aviation emissions by 10% over the next decade.
The national air traffic controller, Nats, has pledged to reduce the amount of carbon dioxide generated in British airspace by 2020. Meeting the benchmark will require new approach paths for airports, greater cooperation with neighbouring air traffic regimes and shorter delays on airport taxi-ways. Nats expects its initiatives to reduce the amount of fuel needed each journey by 10%.
Paul Barron, Nats chief executive, said the target would galvanise the aviation industry in Britain and across Europe as the company seeks help in slashing emissions. "We cannot do this on our own, even though we have set the target independently," he said. "We have got to engage the airports, the airlines, technology companies and our partners around Europe."
Nats is responsible for guiding all civilian flights through British airspace and also handles air traffic control for Britain's largest airports including Heathrow, Gatwick and Manchester. The former state-owned company handled 2.4m flights last year and generated profits of £94.4m.
The aviation industry is under pressure to take action on emissions, with flights accounting for 1.6% of the carbon dioxide emitted worldwide and 5.5% of CO2 generated in Britain. Its biggest defence has been the introduction of fuel-efficient planes such as the A380 superjumbo but global passenger numbers are increasing at such a rate that the CO2 reduction is being more than wiped out by rising demand for flights.
