The man accused of fatally shooting a 21-year-old expectant father outside a family party on Saturday in Paulsboro turned himself in to Atlantic City police on Tuesday.
Anthony Kille, 20, was charged with murder, possession of a weapon for an unlawful purpose, possession of a weapon and receiving stolen property, Gloucester County Prosecutor Charles Fiore said.
Kille is accused of killing 21-year-old Davontae Randall, a former star wrestler at Paulsboro High School, just days after family said Kille confronted Randall about a close call he had on the road.
Randall was driving his girlfriend Felisha Daniels when they came close to colliding with a car at an intersection in Swedesboro, Daniels said. She said Kille argued with and threatened Randall.
Days later, Kille showed up at a family party at Randall's mother's house on Buck Street in Paulsboro, according to the probable cause affidavit in the case.
According to that document, surveillance video captured Kille arguing with Randall and vowing to come back with a gun. About a minute later, he returned with a semi-automatic handgun, and shot Randall in the chest.
Convict allegedly couldn't let road rage go. Former star wrestler paid with his life
Since the murder Saturday, the Gloucester County Prosecutor's Office Fugitive Unit, Paulsboro police and the U.S. Marshals searched for Kille, Fiore said in a news statement. He credited Atlantic City police for their role in the arrest.
Also on Tuesday, NJ Advance Media learned that the night of Randall's death is not the first time Kille has been accused of violating his home confinement.
After he was charged with involvement in a 21-person drug ring in Paulsboro -- he allegedly sold heroin to an undercover detective in that case -- Kille was placed on home confinement and ordered to stay at a house on Delaware Street in Paulsboro. He was not electronically monitored.
But hours after Kille left pretrial services on July 16, authorities said he left the house.
Kille was spotted at a Heritage's convenience store in Paulsboro, in an argument with a man who said he knew Kille from childhood, according to court records. That man told police Kille threatened him and mentioned he was carrying a gun.
