As President Barack Obama tried to calm a divided nation, and Donald Trump encouraged our country's adversaries to engage in illegal espionage, and the United Kingdom is slowly realizing in hindsight how shitty their Brexit decision was, and Europe and the Middle East live under threat of constant terror attacks and the world prepares to launch the most fucked up Olympics in recent memory, Radiohead returned to the United States.
"Every day you open your newspaper, you open your phone and some crazy broken shit is going on. We really, really need to wake up," frontman Thom Yorke told the crowd at Madison Square Garden on the second of the band's two-night run in New York. It's a warning—one that Radiohead has been giving us for decades. As if to illustrate his point, the band jumped into "Planet Telex," the wavering intro track from their breakthrough 1995 album, The Bends. "Everything is broken," Yorke reminds us on the song. "You can crush it, but it's always near."
It's something that Radiohead has never let us forget, and now, during the most vile election in American history, they're here to reiterate their point. This sounds bleak, and maybe that's just reality, but it's a message Radiohead had in the '90s that has evolved from angsty alt-rock to patient, focused mediations on modern guitar music and the end of the world.
And if the world ends, I want to be at a fucking Radiohead concert.
Radiohead started at the beginning of the end on Wednesday night, playing the first five songs of the band's newest album, A Moon Shaped Pool. "This is a low-flying panic attack," Yorke sings as Jonny Greenwood carefully and beautifully bows his guitar. This is. And, again, it's a warning. But live, the material off Radiohead's most restrained album to date has a greater sense of urgency.
More importantly, the band somehow turns what is a slow and pensive album into something, well, fun. It's not at all depressing—though, one would be right to imagine this album would be. Through the sheer mastery of their craft, Radiohead knows how to burst into whirlwinds of vocal loops and synths. The drums—Phil Selway is joined by a second percussionist on all the new material—click forward like industrial machinery. And Greenwood does whatever magic he does over to the side in his little laboratory of electronics, guitars, and keyboards.
In a time when politicians want to convince us that something simple is actually difficult, Radiohead makes something extremely complex sound effortless. A Moon Shaped Pool is the year's most challenging album, and that's never more obvious than seeing the band recreate this material live. With every Radiohead album comes an unspoken promise that they'll be able to recreate the songs in concert as they sound on the album. They did it with Kid A when no one thought it was possible. And A Moon Shaped Pool is such a lush, balanced, and layered album, it sounds like it should take months just to record one song properly. Yet, somehow, the band captures that almost flawlessly.
