Today, we had an old friend make an appearance...the lake breeze.
Milwaukee had a high of 55, meanwhile, Watertown in Jefferson county...nearly 80!
The lake breeze develops in light wind regimes because the Earth warms so much more quickly than the lake water.
The reason for this is in the chemical make up of water versus Earth and all the things and objects on Earth...or the ground is drastically different.
Basically, it takes a LOT more energy to warm the lake by 1 degree than it does the land.
So locally, the really warm ground forms a local area of "lower pressure" because on this scale, the warmer air over the ground is less dense in comparison to the colder water out over the lake.
Once the lower pressure develops, air naturally wants to move from higher pressure to lower pressure.
In this case...from the lake...to the land...the lake breeze.
So will there be a lake breeze this weekend? Well yes...and no.
More accurately, there could be a breeze with an easterly component, but I do not think we will get a true lake breeze regime like what we had Friday because the large scale wind flow will be strong enough to hold the lake chill at bay.
Now having said that, with southeast winds to south for Saturday, for a time in the morning the breeze will be off of Lake Michigan...trying to cool lakeside locations.
However, by the afternoon, winds are expected to go straight south, and will allow most spots to soar into the 70s.
The exception being Sheboygan, Port Washington, and Racine, where even a south wind ushers in lake modified air, due to how the land juts out into the lake a bit.
